# ANTI-REGRESSION POSITIVE — C3, an AXIS pin. Ratified by D10(b) ADDENDUM 3
# (`docs/define-gorget/decisions.md`, end of the LOG).
#
# `Pair(!v, v[0])` moves `v` at arg 0 and then reads a sub-place of it at arg 1.
# Left-to-right evaluation makes that a use-after-MOVE, and the reference-grade
# diagnostic is the LIVENESS one:
#
#     error[E_UseAfterMove]: use of moved value `v`
#
# NOT `E_BorrowConflict`. The distinction is load-bearing: the aggregate-init
# mover exemption (see `sound_ctor_mover_sibling_accept.gg`) is safe precisely
# BECAUSE the genuinely hazardous ordering is already caught on the liveness
# axis. If a chokepoint rebuild makes this fail with the aliasing error instead,
# the move-tracker silently lost a case and the exemption's justification
# evaporates with it — this fixture is what catches that.
#
# CORRECT TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg): rejects with `E_UseAfterMove`.
#
# ORACLE (ggdef, measured this session): ggdef also rejects, but on the OTHER
# axis — `error[E_BorrowConflict]: cannot pass `!v` and `v` in the same call`.
# Both lanes refuse the program, so there is no soundness divergence, but the
# CODES disagree and the ledger is explicit that the liveness code is the
# correct one. Filed as a lane-alignment note rather than pinned here: this
# fixture asserts production's `E_UseAfterMove`, which is the ratified answer.
struct Pair:
    Vector[String] rest
    String s

void main():
    Vector[String] v = Vector[String]()
    v.push("alpha-heap-forced-payload")
    Pair p = Pair(!v, v[0])
    print(p.s)
